4
00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.312
Lesson five point four asked whether drum

5
00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:13.505
hits land with guitar notes more often

6
00:00:13.505 --> 00:00:14.180
than chance.

7
00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:16.823
A fixed loop that never listens tied the

8
00:00:16.823 --> 00:00:19.354
real drummers on that: on seven hundred

9
00:00:19.354 --> 00:00:21.716
and seventy Rock songs, own drums minus

10
00:00:21.716 --> 00:00:23.178
the loop came out at zero.

11
00:00:23.515 --> 00:00:24.303
No difference.

12
00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:26.858
So following asks about change instead.

13
00:00:27.184 --> 00:00:29.575
When the guitar moves into a new section,

14
00:00:29.575 --> 00:00:32.021
does the drum part change too, and does it

15
00:00:32.021 --> 00:00:34.032
mostly stay put the rest of the time?

16
00:00:34.729 --> 00:00:36.113
That needs two measurements.

17
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.585
Where the guitar changes section: this

18
00:00:38.585 --> 00:00:38.931
lesson.

19
00:00:39.227 --> 00:00:41.155
And how much the drums change from one

20
00:00:41.155 --> 00:00:43.577
window, two bars, to the next: the next

21
00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:43.923
lesson.

22
00:00:47.357 --> 00:00:49.847
Everything here works on windows: two bars

23
00:00:49.847 --> 00:00:52.131
of four-four, cut into sixteenth notes,

24
00:00:52.131 --> 00:00:53.635
which makes thirty-two slots.

25
00:00:54.075 --> 00:00:56.623
Here's one window of a made-up rock beat:

26
00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.899
kick on one and three, snare on two and

27
00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:01.230
four, closed hi-hat on every eighth note.

28
00:01:01.681 --> 00:01:03.841
The battery files the nine drums into

29
00:01:03.841 --> 00:01:04.579
three groups.

30
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:05.829
Low is the kick.

31
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.216
Mid is the snare and the three toms.

32
00:01:08.557 --> 00:01:10.886
High is the hi-hats, the crash and the

33
00:01:10.886 --> 00:01:11.170
ride.

34
00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:14.587
Each group becomes one row of thirty-two:

35
00:01:14.587 --> 00:01:17.093
a slot is on if any drum in the group hits

36
00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:17.268
it.

37
00:01:17.618 --> 00:01:20.008
Two cymbals in one slot still count once.

38
00:01:21.066 --> 00:01:23.768
Lay the three rows end to end, low, mid,

39
00:01:23.768 --> 00:01:26.182
high, and you have ninety-six on-or-off

40
00:01:26.182 --> 00:01:28.769
bits: the window's signature, written

41
00:01:28.769 --> 00:01:29.401
sigma of w.

42
00:01:29.918 --> 00:01:32.332
This one has twenty-four bits on: four

43
00:01:32.332 --> 00:01:34.252
kick, four snare, sixteen hats.

44
00:01:34.582 --> 00:01:36.282
Only whether a drum hit counts.

45
00:01:36.612 --> 00:01:39.190
How loud it was, and how far off the grid,

46
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:39.849
never enter.

47
00:01:40.489 --> 00:01:43.096
The guitar gets a plainer fingerprint: one

48
00:01:43.096 --> 00:01:45.323
row of thirty-two, a bit on wherever a

49
00:01:45.323 --> 00:01:45.974
note starts.

50
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.603
This verse riff has ten.

51
00:01:51.097 --> 00:01:53.057
To compare two windows, we count bits.

52
00:01:53.351 --> 00:01:55.508
Here's the verse beat again, and a chorus

53
00:01:55.508 --> 00:01:57.909
beat: a busier kick, and the hi-hat drops

54
00:01:57.909 --> 00:01:58.743
to quarter notes.

55
00:01:59.235 --> 00:02:01.469
Fourteen bits are on in both: two kicks,

56
00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:02.865
four snares, and eight hats.

57
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:05.804
Thirty are on in either one: twenty-four

58
00:02:05.804 --> 00:02:07.913
plus twenty, minus the fourteen we'd

59
00:02:07.913 --> 00:02:09.102
otherwise count twice.

60
00:02:09.589 --> 00:02:11.852
That ratio is the Jaccard similarity, J.

61
00:02:12.176 --> 00:02:14.332
On top, the number of bits on in both

62
00:02:14.332 --> 00:02:14.763
windows.

63
00:02:15.086 --> 00:02:17.134
Underneath, the number on in either.

64
00:02:17.458 --> 00:02:19.937
Here, fourteen over thirty: zero point

65
00:02:19.937 --> 00:02:20.745
four six seven.

66
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.906
Turned round, one minus J says how much

67
00:02:23.906 --> 00:02:26.412
changed: zero point five three three.

68
00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.978
The next lesson uses exactly this to

69
00:02:28.978 --> 00:02:30.052
measure the drums.

70
00:02:30.531 --> 00:02:32.804
Notice what the ratio leaves out: the

71
00:02:32.804 --> 00:02:34.865
sixty-six slots that are empty in both.

72
00:02:35.182 --> 00:02:36.663
Most of a window is silence.

73
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.200
If shared silence counted as agreement,

74
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.314
any two sparse windows would look alike.

75
00:02:41.632 --> 00:02:44.169
J runs from zero, nothing shared, to one,

76
00:02:44.169 --> 00:02:44.698
identical.

77
00:02:45.015 --> 00:02:46.812
Two empty windows score one, by

78
00:02:46.812 --> 00:02:47.393
convention.

79
00:02:48.863 --> 00:02:51.052
The guitar's rows use the same Jaccard.

80
00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:53.073
The verse riff has ten onsets.

81
00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.767
The chorus strums every eighth note:

82
00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:56.217
sixteen.

83
00:02:56.683 --> 00:02:59.084
Eight onsets are shared, and eighteen are

84
00:02:59.084 --> 00:03:01.649
on in either: J is eight over eighteen,

85
00:03:01.649 --> 00:03:03.068
zero point four four four.

86
00:03:03.396 --> 00:03:04.324
Keep that number.

87
00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:05.415
It comes back.

88
00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.397
Now a whole made-up song, sixteen windows:

89
00:03:11.397 --> 00:03:13.403
six of the verse riff, five of chorus

90
00:03:13.403 --> 00:03:15.459
strumming, and five of the verse again.

91
00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:18.177
Compare every window's guitar with every

92
00:03:18.177 --> 00:03:20.446
other window's, and write each Jaccard

93
00:03:20.446 --> 00:03:22.327
into a grid, sixteen by sixteen.

94
00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:26.035
Row i, column j holds S i j: the Jaccard

95
00:03:26.035 --> 00:03:28.665
of the guitar in window i and window j.

96
00:03:29.232 --> 00:03:31.027
The diagonal is each window against

97
00:03:31.027 --> 00:03:32.373
itself, so it's all ones.

98
00:03:32.783 --> 00:03:35.175
And the grid is symmetric: window i

99
00:03:35.175 --> 00:03:37.333
against j is the same as j against i.

100
00:03:37.859 --> 00:03:40.198
Verse against verse is one, because the

101
00:03:40.198 --> 00:03:42.592
riff repeats exactly, and chorus against

102
00:03:42.592 --> 00:03:43.594
chorus is one too.

103
00:03:43.928 --> 00:03:46.099
Verse against chorus is the zero point

104
00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:47.603
four four four from before.

105
00:03:47.937 --> 00:03:50.052
And the bright corners show the verse

106
00:03:50.052 --> 00:03:52.558
coming back: windows zero to five match

107
00:03:52.558 --> 00:03:54.005
windows eleven to fifteen.

108
00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:56.732
In this made-up song every cell is one of

109
00:03:56.732 --> 00:03:57.593
just two values.

110
00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:00.179
Real songs are messier, but the squares

111
00:04:00.179 --> 00:04:02.656
along the diagonal are what we look for:

112
00:04:02.656 --> 00:04:04.326
they are the guitar's sections.

113
00:04:07.763 --> 00:04:09.760
What does a section change look like in

114
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:10.259
this grid?

115
00:04:10.558 --> 00:04:12.804
Take the change into the chorus, at window

116
00:04:12.804 --> 00:04:13.004
six.

117
00:04:13.447 --> 00:04:15.709
Just before it, four verse windows, all

118
00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:16.017
alike.

119
00:04:16.326 --> 00:04:18.588
Just after it, four chorus windows, all

120
00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:18.896
alike.

121
00:04:19.205 --> 00:04:21.518
Across it, verse against chorus: they

122
00:04:21.518 --> 00:04:21.878
differ.

123
00:04:22.313 --> 00:04:24.369
Two bright squares on the diagonal, two

124
00:04:24.369 --> 00:04:25.545
dimmer ones beside them.

125
00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:27.259
That's the pattern to detect.

126
00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:30.476
A checkerboard of weights finds that

127
00:04:30.476 --> 00:04:30.889
pattern.

128
00:04:31.198 --> 00:04:33.622
It's Foote's kernel, after Jonathan Foote,

129
00:04:33.622 --> 00:04:35.634
who proposed it for finding changes in

130
00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:36.769
audio in two thousand.

131
00:04:37.078 --> 00:04:39.400
Its half-width is k, four windows, so it

132
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.618
covers eight by eight, with the boundary

133
00:04:41.618 --> 00:04:42.598
through the middle.

134
00:04:43.043 --> 00:04:45.309
Each row and each column gets a weight, c

135
00:04:45.309 --> 00:04:45.566
of u.

136
00:04:45.875 --> 00:04:48.244
Here u counts windows from the boundary:

137
00:04:48.244 --> 00:04:50.561
minus four to minus one before it, zero to

138
00:04:50.561 --> 00:04:51.179
three after.

139
00:04:51.488 --> 00:04:53.651
Window u's middle sits u plus a half from

140
00:04:53.651 --> 00:04:56.277
the boundary: a half, one and a half, two

141
00:04:56.277 --> 00:04:57.874
and a half, three and a half.

142
00:04:58.347 --> 00:05:00.598
The sign is minus before the boundary and

143
00:05:00.598 --> 00:05:01.187
plus after.

144
00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:03.706
Then a bell-shaped taper, width k over

145
00:05:03.706 --> 00:05:06.117
two, that is two windows, so the windows

146
00:05:06.117 --> 00:05:08.046
nearest the boundary weigh the most.

147
00:05:08.481 --> 00:05:10.992
Going outward the weights are zero point

148
00:05:10.992 --> 00:05:13.686
nine seven, zero point seven five, zero

149
00:05:13.686 --> 00:05:16.381
point four six and zero point two two, on

150
00:05:16.381 --> 00:05:16.993
each side.

151
00:05:17.495 --> 00:05:20.048
Each cell of the kernel is its row weight

152
00:05:20.048 --> 00:05:22.906
times its column weight, divided by Z, the

153
00:05:22.906 --> 00:05:25.581
total size of all sixty-four products, so

154
00:05:25.581 --> 00:05:27.040
the sizes add up to one.

155
00:05:27.594 --> 00:05:29.190
Before times before is plus.

156
00:05:29.532 --> 00:05:31.015
After times after is plus.

157
00:05:31.357 --> 00:05:32.953
Before times after is minus.

158
00:05:33.295 --> 00:05:34.663
That's the checkerboard.

159
00:05:35.006 --> 00:05:37.514
The biggest weights, zero point zero four

160
00:05:37.514 --> 00:05:38.768
one, hug the centre.

161
00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:41.334
The corners carry zero point zero zero

162
00:05:41.334 --> 00:05:41.562
two.

163
00:05:42.018 --> 00:05:42.941
Add every weight.

164
00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:45.547
The plus cells sum to a half, the minus

165
00:05:45.547 --> 00:05:47.827
cells to minus a half, and the total is

166
00:05:47.827 --> 00:05:48.532
exactly zero.

167
00:05:49.024 --> 00:05:50.001
That's deliberate.

168
00:05:50.327 --> 00:05:52.282
Lay the kernel over a stretch where

169
00:05:52.282 --> 00:05:54.726
nothing changes, all ones, and it scores

170
00:05:54.726 --> 00:05:55.432
exactly zero.

171
00:05:55.758 --> 00:05:57.278
It answers only to contrast.

172
00:05:57.739 --> 00:05:59.234
One detail for the report.

173
00:05:59.579 --> 00:06:01.821
The explainer page prints the taper at

174
00:06:01.821 --> 00:06:04.408
whole-number offsets, which would give the

175
00:06:04.408 --> 00:06:06.075
row at zero a weight of zero.

176
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.834
The code uses the half steps you see here.

177
00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.384
Novelty, written nu of t, measures how

178
00:06:15.384 --> 00:06:17.366
much window t looks like the start of

179
00:06:17.366 --> 00:06:18.097
something new.

180
00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:21.086
Lay the kernel on the grid, centred just

181
00:06:21.086 --> 00:06:23.582
before window t, multiply each weight by

182
00:06:23.582 --> 00:06:26.021
the similarity under it, and add up all

183
00:06:26.021 --> 00:06:27.182
sixty-four products.

184
00:06:27.625 --> 00:06:29.770
At window six the plus quarters lie on

185
00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:31.915
verse against verse and chorus against

186
00:06:31.915 --> 00:06:34.555
chorus: all ones, under weights that sum

187
00:06:34.555 --> 00:06:35.105
to a half.

188
00:06:35.614 --> 00:06:38.103
The minus quarters, minus a half in all,

189
00:06:38.103 --> 00:06:40.592
lie on verse against chorus: zero point

190
00:06:40.592 --> 00:06:41.422
four four four.

191
00:06:41.887 --> 00:06:44.481
So nu of six is one minus zero point four

192
00:06:44.481 --> 00:06:47.506
four four, over two: zero point two seven

193
00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:47.877
eight.

194
00:06:48.403 --> 00:06:50.586
That's the rule for any clean change,

195
00:06:50.586 --> 00:06:53.098
where s is the similarity across it: one

196
00:06:53.098 --> 00:06:54.189
minus s, over two.

197
00:06:54.517 --> 00:06:56.974
If the two sections are the same, s is one

198
00:06:56.974 --> 00:06:58.448
and there's no peak at all.

199
00:06:58.775 --> 00:07:01.123
If they share nothing, s is zero and the

200
00:07:01.123 --> 00:07:02.815
peak reaches its top, a half.

201
00:07:03.326 --> 00:07:05.616
Now slide the kernel down the diagonal,

202
00:07:05.616 --> 00:07:08.015
one window at a time, from window four to

203
00:07:08.015 --> 00:07:08.779
window twelve.

204
00:07:09.656 --> 00:07:11.653
Near the ends the kernel doesn't fit

205
00:07:11.653 --> 00:07:14.189
inside the grid, so windows zero to three,

206
00:07:14.189 --> 00:07:16.132
and thirteen to fifteen, get zero.

207
00:07:16.588 --> 00:07:18.943
The curve peaks at window six and again at

208
00:07:18.943 --> 00:07:21.298
window eleven, both zero point two seven

209
00:07:21.298 --> 00:07:21.627
eight.

210
00:07:21.955 --> 00:07:24.529
Between them, in the middle of the chorus,

211
00:07:24.529 --> 00:07:26.829
it barely moves: zero point zero one.

212
00:07:31.037 --> 00:07:33.292
Two rules turn the curve into boundaries.

213
00:07:33.622 --> 00:07:35.932
First, a candidate must be a peak, at

214
00:07:35.932 --> 00:07:38.352
least as high as both its neighbours, and

215
00:07:38.352 --> 00:07:40.607
stand strictly above zero point one five.

216
00:07:41.123 --> 00:07:43.474
The shoulders at zero point zero nine nine

217
00:07:43.474 --> 00:07:46.043
aren't peaks: each has a taller neighbour.

218
00:07:46.371 --> 00:07:48.612
In this song only windows six and eleven

219
00:07:48.612 --> 00:07:51.181
are peaks, and both clear the line, so the

220
00:07:51.181 --> 00:07:52.875
threshold removes nothing here.

221
00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:55.881
Second, peaks are kept tallest first, and

222
00:07:55.881 --> 00:07:58.424
never closer than k, four windows, so one

223
00:07:58.424 --> 00:08:00.083
change can't be counted twice.

224
00:08:00.414 --> 00:08:02.681
Six and eleven are five apart, so both

225
00:08:02.681 --> 00:08:02.957
stay.

226
00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:05.887
These are the boundaries, B: windows six

227
00:08:05.887 --> 00:08:08.200
and eleven, each the first window of a new

228
00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:08.611
section.

229
00:08:09.073 --> 00:08:11.256
Notice who decided that: the guitar alone.

230
00:08:11.534 --> 00:08:12.974
The drums were never looked at.

231
00:08:13.413 --> 00:08:15.574
And the matrix compared every window with

232
00:08:15.574 --> 00:08:17.374
every other, later ones included.

233
00:08:17.683 --> 00:08:19.895
The detector sees the whole song before it

234
00:08:19.895 --> 00:08:20.769
marks a boundary.

235
00:08:21.078 --> 00:08:23.135
That's fine for scoring a finished song.

236
00:08:23.444 --> 00:08:25.759
It matters in the next lesson, because our

237
00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:27.045
drummers can't see ahead.

238
00:08:28.305 --> 00:08:30.466
And when there's nothing to find, there's

239
00:08:30.466 --> 00:08:30.957
no number.

240
00:08:31.252 --> 00:08:33.315
A song shorter than ten windows, twenty

241
00:08:33.315 --> 00:08:35.574
bars, or one with no peak above the line,

242
00:08:35.574 --> 00:08:37.489
gets no reading, never a made-up one.

243
00:08:37.784 --> 00:08:39.994
On the committed Rock check, fifty-five of

244
00:08:39.994 --> 00:08:42.106
two hundred and twenty-three songs gave no

245
00:08:42.106 --> 00:08:43.580
reading: about a quarter.

246
00:08:45.106 --> 00:08:47.622
So: fingerprint each window, compare them

247
00:08:47.622 --> 00:08:49.881
all by Jaccard, slide a checkerboard down

248
00:08:49.881 --> 00:08:51.934
the diagonal, and keep the tall peaks.

249
00:08:52.242 --> 00:08:53.731
The guitar picks the moments.

250
00:08:54.039 --> 00:08:56.298
Next: how the drums are judged at them.

